WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.872
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.872 --> 00:00:08.080
DANNA KELMER: Hello everyone.

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:08.622
Good morning.

00:00:08.622 --> 00:00:09.420
My name is Danna.

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:10.920
I'm a software
engineer on the Bazel

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:13.590
Team at Google in New York.

00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:16.620
Today, we are going
to talk about problems

00:00:16.620 --> 00:00:19.230
that certain organizations
start to encounter

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:22.320
as their company and
their projects grow,

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:25.170
in particular with-- in
relation to build and test

00:00:25.170 --> 00:00:28.170
times, and then go over
the ways in which the

00:00:28.170 --> 00:00:31.620
build tool Bazel can help
alleviate some of those pains.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:35.130
And finish up with a testimonial
from the Angular team

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:37.660
about how it's been
working for them.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:42.780
So why might an
organization need Bazel?

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:46.740
Certain problems start to arise
as your organization grows.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:49.320
Your projects start
to grow and the needs

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:52.590
of your infrastructure start
to suffer with the load

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:55.570
that it requires, and in
turn, this might affect your--

00:00:55.570 --> 00:00:58.110
the development
experience of the people

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:00.090
at your organization.

00:01:00.090 --> 00:01:03.630
And in part, this
happens because, as you

00:01:03.630 --> 00:01:08.310
have more developers, they
start to make more changes.

00:01:08.310 --> 00:01:11.610
And more changes means that you
need to run all of your tests

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:12.580
far more often.

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:16.440
So if you wanted to run all of
your tests before every submit,

00:01:16.440 --> 00:01:19.210
that starts to increase
as you have more people.

00:01:19.210 --> 00:01:22.530
The second thing that affects
this is as the feature space

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:25.680
starts to grow, this obviously
increases just the sheer number

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.400
of tests that need to run.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:30.450
So this is more load that
must happen with every change.

00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:33.840
And the final part is as
your project becomes larger,

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.980
each individual integration test
requires a ton of compute power

00:01:37.980 --> 00:01:39.360
just for a single one.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:41.340
And so it starts to
become, very quickly,

00:01:41.340 --> 00:01:46.380
infeasible to run all of
your tests on every submit.

00:01:46.380 --> 00:01:50.400
So basically, many
organizations tend to give up

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.200
on integration testing.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:55.140
And this means that
instead, they'll

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:59.520
break up their projects into a
number of microservices where

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:03.060
each service is responsible
for its own set of tests,

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:06.270
and then those will
run on every submit.

00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:09.930
And you'll only ever run your
substantial integration tests

00:02:09.930 --> 00:02:12.130
when you attempt to release.

00:02:12.130 --> 00:02:15.030
So this is an example
of a way that you

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:16.950
might break up some project.

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:19.115
And what this means is
that when you're releasing,

00:02:19.115 --> 00:02:20.490
that's the first
time that you're

00:02:20.490 --> 00:02:24.240
going to encounter problems
with the interaction

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:26.040
between these different
microservices that

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:27.694
have been working separately.

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:34.050
All right, so what is Bazel?

00:02:34.050 --> 00:02:36.360
And how is it going
to help this problem

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:39.180
and enable and empower
your organization

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:41.460
to run all of these
tests as often

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.700
as possible on every commit?

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:47.560
So Bazel is a free
and open source

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:49.410
automated build and test tool.

00:02:49.410 --> 00:02:52.320
This tool has been used at
Google for nearly the past 15

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.460
years to build all
of its source code.

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:00.090
And I'm going to go over
the properties of Bazel that

00:03:00.090 --> 00:03:03.630
enable this, that enable
you to run all of your tests

00:03:03.630 --> 00:03:07.630
across your entire organization
as often as you'd like.

00:03:07.630 --> 00:03:10.890
So the first property is
that Bazel is hermetic.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:14.220
This means that Bazel
is aware of all inputs

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:15.870
before the build begins.

00:03:15.870 --> 00:03:18.180
So of course, this includes
all of your source files

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:20.650
and everything that
would need to be built.

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:23.070
But this also includes
any tool, for example,

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:25.885
that would need to be used
over the course of the build.

00:03:28.590 --> 00:03:31.470
And in turn, this
hermeticity directly

00:03:31.470 --> 00:03:34.410
lends itself to Bazel
being incremental.

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:38.130
And so because Bazel
is aware of all inputs,

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:40.890
for any change you make,
Bazel can very easily

00:03:40.890 --> 00:03:42.840
calculate the minimal
amount of work

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:46.110
that needs to be done in order
to create your finished build

00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:46.960
product.

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:48.780
And so, if you make
a small change,

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:50.670
you should expect that
the amount of work

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.870
that Bazel will do
will be proportional,

00:03:52.870 --> 00:03:54.550
that it will be small.

00:03:54.550 --> 00:03:57.450
So I'm going to
demonstrate that.

00:04:03.430 --> 00:04:03.930
All right.

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:05.645
So here's an example.

00:04:05.645 --> 00:04:06.270
We're starting.

00:04:06.270 --> 00:04:08.910
We're asking Bazel to
build all the tests.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:13.140
And, just to start, we see that
everything is fully cached.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:16.120
So this is imagining a scenario
that you just did a build,

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:16.620
right?

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:17.245
We're starting.

00:04:17.245 --> 00:04:19.890
Everything was already
built, and now we're

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.810
going to make just
a small change.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:25.500
So the change is going to
be to a single test file.

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:30.340
So a test-- a test itself, often
nothing depends on that test.

00:04:30.340 --> 00:04:32.760
So the expectation is that
when you make the change

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:36.180
to this single test, that only
that single test will have

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:39.480
to be rebuilt and retested.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:42.150
So here's an example test file.

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:47.310
We're going to add an additional
assertion to this test

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:52.680
to make sure that everything,
or that this thing is not null.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:54.570
And then, again, what
we're going to do

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:59.010
is we're going to ask
Bazel to retest everything.

00:04:59.010 --> 00:05:01.380
But the expectation is
that Bazel will be smarter,

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:03.750
and it will only
rebuild that single test

00:05:03.750 --> 00:05:05.940
and rerun that single test.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:10.620
So Bazel is working
and beautiful.

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:12.030
Everything is cached.

00:05:12.030 --> 00:05:15.640
But we see it executed
one out of 174 tests.

00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:17.520
So that's the one
test that we edited.

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:19.110
Everything else is still cached.

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:26.020
All right.

00:05:26.020 --> 00:05:30.320
The next property of Bazel
is that it's deterministic.

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:33.490
So Bazel uses the checksum
of all of the inputs

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:35.710
to the build in order to
determine whether or not

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:36.880
they've changed.

00:05:36.880 --> 00:05:41.380
And what this means then is that
Bazel can very safely cache any

00:05:41.380 --> 00:05:44.920
intermediary build
artifacts that it produces.

00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:46.510
And then instead
of redoing the work

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:49.690
to do it, if it knows that none
of the inputs have changed,

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:51.700
you can safely retrieve
the output artifact

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:52.480
from this cache.

00:05:55.130 --> 00:05:58.040
And this is a huge
opportunity for optimization

00:05:58.040 --> 00:05:59.900
at your organization
because you're not

00:05:59.900 --> 00:06:01.700
bound to keep this cache local.

00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:03.620
You can share this
crop-- this cache

00:06:03.620 --> 00:06:07.070
across your entire organization,
and all of your developers

00:06:07.070 --> 00:06:10.130
can populate this cache, and
not only your developers,

00:06:10.130 --> 00:06:13.430
but your CI system can
also populate this cache.

00:06:13.430 --> 00:06:16.810
And then whenever your
developers are doing builds,

00:06:16.810 --> 00:06:19.700
Bazel can then check
the cache and see

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:22.200
if the artifacts that it
needs are already present.

00:06:22.200 --> 00:06:26.310
And if yes, simply retrieve them
without doing any work at all.

00:06:26.310 --> 00:06:30.905
So this is some example of how
this cache might be shared.

00:06:30.905 --> 00:06:33.030
And of course what this
means is that, for example,

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:35.430
the first built of the
day will also be fast.

00:06:35.430 --> 00:06:37.320
If you say have your
cache, and it's--

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:37.820
sorry.

00:06:37.820 --> 00:06:40.322
If you have your CI system
populating this cache,

00:06:40.322 --> 00:06:42.530
then when your developers
get to work in the morning,

00:06:42.530 --> 00:06:43.880
they do their first build.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:46.430
That build will probably be
trivially fast because almost

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:49.940
all artifacts will be cached,
very similar to the example

00:06:49.940 --> 00:06:51.710
above before I change the test.

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:56.610
All right.

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:59.290
And this cache can be anywhere.

00:06:59.290 --> 00:07:01.740
So you can use Google Hub--

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:05.220
Google Cloud Storage in order to
have this cache be in the cloud

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:06.030
not hosted by you.

00:07:06.030 --> 00:07:07.770
But you can also
set up this cache

00:07:07.770 --> 00:07:10.710
to be hosted on premises
for everyone to use.

00:07:13.642 --> 00:07:16.100
And the final property of Bazel
that we're going to go over

00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:17.900
is that it's fully cloud ready.

00:07:17.900 --> 00:07:19.988
And so because of all
the previous properties

00:07:19.988 --> 00:07:21.530
that we talked about,
its hermeticity

00:07:21.530 --> 00:07:23.930
on its determinism,
not only can you

00:07:23.930 --> 00:07:26.480
store each of the
intermediary artifacts,

00:07:26.480 --> 00:07:30.500
but you can also do
the work on the cloud.

00:07:30.500 --> 00:07:33.740
All of the build actions
can happen in the cloud.

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:42.800
So we're going to look at
an example of another build.

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:48.730
So in this example,
in this example,

00:07:48.730 --> 00:07:51.580
as before, we're imagining we're
starting from a clean slate.

00:07:51.580 --> 00:07:53.200
Everything has been cached.

00:07:53.200 --> 00:07:57.740
But this time, instead
of changing a test file,

00:07:57.740 --> 00:07:59.680
this is editing a
source file that

00:07:59.680 --> 00:08:01.900
is at the very bottom
of the project.

00:08:01.900 --> 00:08:03.430
Nearly everything
in this project

00:08:03.430 --> 00:08:05.270
depends on this source file.

00:08:05.270 --> 00:08:08.230
And so, now you're going to
expect a full cache miss.

00:08:08.230 --> 00:08:10.540
You're making a unique
change to your project.

00:08:10.540 --> 00:08:14.920
And so there's no way that any
artifacts will be in the cache.

00:08:14.920 --> 00:08:18.730
And so Bazel will do
its incremental build,

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:22.420
but the incremental build
still is a lot of work.

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:24.410
And so it's going
to start working,

00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:27.220
but now you're bound by
the local compute power

00:08:27.220 --> 00:08:28.372
that you have.

00:08:28.372 --> 00:08:29.830
So we're actually
going to let this

00:08:29.830 --> 00:08:31.038
keep running in the meantime.

00:08:36.900 --> 00:08:40.919
But as we said, these builds
can be moved to the cloud

00:08:40.919 --> 00:08:42.419
so that you're no
longer going to be

00:08:42.419 --> 00:08:45.160
bound by the compute
resources that you have.

00:08:45.160 --> 00:08:53.620
So, to demonstrate that,
we have this project.

00:08:53.620 --> 00:08:56.650
And similarly as
before, we're going

00:08:56.650 --> 00:08:59.980
to start from a clean
fully-cached slate,

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:03.990
and then we're going to make
the same edit to the same files

00:09:03.990 --> 00:09:06.110
that we did before.

00:09:06.110 --> 00:09:09.110
And again, this is a file
that everything depends on,

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:11.140
so Bazel will have
to do all of the work

00:09:11.140 --> 00:09:15.190
to rebuild all of the project
and rerun all of the tests.

00:09:15.190 --> 00:09:17.662
But then when we invoke
Bazel in a moment,

00:09:17.662 --> 00:09:19.120
we're going to see
that we're going

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:23.590
to pass an additional flag
that tells Bazel to execute all

00:09:23.590 --> 00:09:25.430
of the build actions remotely.

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:28.520
So this is a setup that has
been done on this project.

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:30.400
There's a bit of configuration.

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:34.600
And then you pass
this additional--

00:09:34.600 --> 00:09:35.630
that doesn't work.

00:09:35.630 --> 00:09:38.180
Another dash dash config
equals remote flag,

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:41.070
and then Bazel knows to
run these actions remotely.

00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:46.520
And if we see here, we
can see that 150 actions

00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:48.140
are running simultaneously.

00:09:48.140 --> 00:09:52.160
So this project was configured
with 150 remote workers,

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:55.490
and Bazel is
utilizing all of it.

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:58.910
And this means it's
running as many tests

00:09:58.910 --> 00:10:03.050
as the configuration allows for.

00:10:03.050 --> 00:10:06.320
So now what happens
is now that we

00:10:06.320 --> 00:10:08.570
see that the number of
actions running simultaneously

00:10:08.570 --> 00:10:11.420
is dropping, that's simply
because there aren't any more

00:10:11.420 --> 00:10:12.782
tests left to run.

00:10:12.782 --> 00:10:14.240
And so at this
point, you're simply

00:10:14.240 --> 00:10:16.530
bounded by your
slowest running test.

00:10:16.530 --> 00:10:18.860
So let's just go
back at this point,

00:10:18.860 --> 00:10:21.650
142 tests have been
executed so far.

00:10:21.650 --> 00:10:24.560
We go back to the job
that was running locally.

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:28.160
We see that only 20 tests
have been executed in the time

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:29.390
that we've been working.

00:10:35.800 --> 00:10:37.640
A couple of these tests
take a while to run,

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:39.500
so we will move on.

00:10:39.500 --> 00:10:43.450
But we see 154 have
finished so far,

00:10:43.450 --> 00:10:45.460
and they're all
running simultaneously.

00:10:45.460 --> 00:10:48.400
So 14 are still running.

00:10:48.400 --> 00:10:50.190
All right, so that
was a demonstration

00:10:50.190 --> 00:10:52.900
of how Bazel will
run in the cloud.

00:10:52.900 --> 00:10:56.970
And now I'm going to pass it on
to Alex to talk about Angular

00:10:56.970 --> 00:10:59.337
and how they've been using it.

00:10:59.337 --> 00:11:00.420
ALEX EAGLE: Thanks, Danna.

00:11:00.420 --> 00:11:01.710
[APPLAUSE]

00:11:01.710 --> 00:11:03.760
Hi, everybody.

00:11:03.760 --> 00:11:04.680
My name is Alex Eagle.

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:06.430
I work on the Angular
team at Google.

00:11:06.430 --> 00:11:09.150
I'm the lead for our tooling
team, which includes things

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:13.260
like making the tools that our
Angular users, about 1 and 1/2

00:11:13.260 --> 00:11:16.050
million of them, use to
develop their applications,

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:18.480
as well as supporting
our own team

00:11:18.480 --> 00:11:20.258
and our internal
development velocity.

00:11:20.258 --> 00:11:22.050
And I want to tell you
about how much Bazel

00:11:22.050 --> 00:11:24.077
has improved our lives.

00:11:24.077 --> 00:11:25.410
If you haven't heard of Angular.

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:28.830
It's a enterprise-scale
web framework

00:11:28.830 --> 00:11:34.170
for a building very
modern web applications.

00:11:34.170 --> 00:11:38.560
And it is a big project.

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:39.450
So how big is it?

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:45.393
So as Danna pointed out,
the development velocity

00:11:45.393 --> 00:11:47.310
on your project, it
depends on how many people

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:48.440
and how big the tests are.

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:51.243
And you might think
that you might--

00:11:51.243 --> 00:11:53.160
we need to break up your
projects so you don't

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:54.580
run all the tests all the time.

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:55.890
That's really not
what we wanted to do.

00:11:55.890 --> 00:11:57.420
So we wanted to use
Bazel so that we

00:11:57.420 --> 00:11:59.070
could run all of our
tests all the time

00:11:59.070 --> 00:12:01.140
and keep our development
velocity high.

00:12:01.140 --> 00:12:03.803
And our numbers, I just
grabbed these off of GitHub,

00:12:03.803 --> 00:12:05.220
and in the last
30 days, we've had

00:12:05.220 --> 00:12:10.570
92 authors, over 400 commits
touching over 1,000 files.

00:12:10.570 --> 00:12:13.770
So this is, I would say,
a pretty medium scale.

00:12:13.770 --> 00:12:17.065
I know a lot of enterprises
have much bigger apps than this,

00:12:17.065 --> 00:12:18.690
but it is enough of
a scale that we saw

00:12:18.690 --> 00:12:22.700
the problem the Bazel solves.

00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:26.273
So this is what our continuous
integration time looks like.

00:12:26.273 --> 00:12:28.440
You can imagine a developer
on the Angular team, one

00:12:28.440 --> 00:12:30.630
of those 95 people, having
to wait an hour for all

00:12:30.630 --> 00:12:32.120
of the tests to
run, and then they

00:12:32.120 --> 00:12:33.370
find out something was broken.

00:12:33.370 --> 00:12:35.010
And so you can imagine going
through this round trip

00:12:35.010 --> 00:12:35.970
many times.

00:12:35.970 --> 00:12:38.308
This significantly hurt
our development velocity.

00:12:38.308 --> 00:12:40.350
And I want to point out
here that the numbers I'm

00:12:40.350 --> 00:12:42.690
showing here are non
incremental, so this is

00:12:42.690 --> 00:12:45.378
just like the uncached build.

00:12:45.378 --> 00:12:46.920
That's sort of the
detail of the fact

00:12:46.920 --> 00:12:49.320
that we get contributions from
the open source community,

00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:53.110
and so using a [INAUDIBLE] cache
has some security risks for us.

00:12:53.110 --> 00:12:55.665
And so this graph is
showing that 60 minutes is

00:12:55.665 --> 00:12:57.540
before we enabled the
remote build execution,

00:12:57.540 --> 00:13:00.450
the dash dash config equals
remote that Danna showed.

00:13:00.450 --> 00:13:02.490
And today, I just
checked this morning,

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:05.400
and a typical round trip
time is 7 and 1/2 minutes

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:06.600
to run all of the tests.

00:13:09.430 --> 00:13:12.180
So this is a decrease
of 87% on our test time,

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:14.890
and it has made a really big
difference to the Angular team.

00:13:14.890 --> 00:13:16.590
There are other
features of Bazel

00:13:16.590 --> 00:13:18.690
that have helped us out a lot.

00:13:18.690 --> 00:13:22.560
This is easily
worth the investment

00:13:22.560 --> 00:13:26.070
that we put into migrating
our build to Bazel.

00:13:26.070 --> 00:13:29.340
So this talk is about
developing like Google,

00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:32.820
and the Angular
project itself is just

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:35.022
a small part of all
of the Angular work

00:13:35.022 --> 00:13:35.980
that goes on at Google.

00:13:35.980 --> 00:13:39.090
We have a lot of customers
internally, over 600 of them,

00:13:39.090 --> 00:13:43.320
and some of these applications
you've probably used.

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:45.660
And all of these applications
rely heavily on remote

00:13:45.660 --> 00:13:50.040
build execution to allow a
very large number of Google

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:52.560
Developers to be productive
working in these applications.

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:54.477
And yes, we write full
stack integration tests

00:13:54.477 --> 00:13:57.700
and expect them to
run all the time.

00:13:57.700 --> 00:14:00.450
So now that Angular itself
has been successfully ported

00:14:00.450 --> 00:14:02.910
to Bazel, we want to make
it possible for all of you,

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:05.380
if you're using Angular
2.0, to the same thing.

00:14:05.380 --> 00:14:09.570
And so as-- we're developing
an opt-in preview which

00:14:09.570 --> 00:14:12.780
will ship in the next month as
part of version 8 of Angular,

00:14:12.780 --> 00:14:15.270
that will allow all of
our enterprise users

00:14:15.270 --> 00:14:17.718
to be able to opt-in
to using Bazel.

00:14:17.718 --> 00:14:20.010
We will plan to work with
them over the next six months

00:14:20.010 --> 00:14:23.070
to help them find patterns of
porting parts of their builds

00:14:23.070 --> 00:14:25.320
to run under Bazel
and allow them

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:27.790
to use remote build execution.

00:14:27.790 --> 00:14:31.080
And I also want to point out
that our plan for version 8

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:33.810
is that, if you don't have
a very large scale project,

00:14:33.810 --> 00:14:35.670
there's actually no
porting required.

00:14:35.670 --> 00:14:38.080
Bazel can just drop
into your project.

00:14:38.080 --> 00:14:40.500
It's really for the
enterprise use cases

00:14:40.500 --> 00:14:45.142
that some extra migration
work will be needed.

00:14:45.142 --> 00:14:47.100
So there's one more thing
I want to talk about.

00:14:47.100 --> 00:14:49.380
We showed how build
and test can benefit

00:14:49.380 --> 00:14:53.033
a lot from incrementality
and can be run by Bazel,

00:14:53.033 --> 00:14:54.450
but we're at the
cloud conference,

00:14:54.450 --> 00:14:56.680
and we are all thinking
about deploying services.

00:14:56.680 --> 00:14:59.865
So let's talk about
deployment for a minute.

00:14:59.865 --> 00:15:02.490
A year and a half ago, when the
Angular team switched to Bazel,

00:15:02.490 --> 00:15:05.220
we also switched how we
do our own deployments.

00:15:05.220 --> 00:15:09.510
And so, we run basically a loop
over our packages of just doing

00:15:09.510 --> 00:15:12.300
Bazel deploy of each one.

00:15:12.300 --> 00:15:14.490
That's not that interesting
because we're a library.

00:15:14.490 --> 00:15:15.948
And so our deployment
is a shipping

00:15:15.948 --> 00:15:18.347
an artifact to a registry.

00:15:18.347 --> 00:15:20.430
What's more interesting
is that we have an example

00:15:20.430 --> 00:15:24.210
application that looks like an
enterprise Angular app would,

00:15:24.210 --> 00:15:27.000
and as Danna showed,
only the change packages

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:28.450
get rebuilt when we do a deploy.

00:15:28.450 --> 00:15:30.825
So Bazel's smart enough to
know what work it needs to do.

00:15:30.825 --> 00:15:32.970
But then, the changes
that are produced by that

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:35.423
can go into a new layer
in the docker image.

00:15:35.423 --> 00:15:36.840
And then only that
new layer needs

00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:39.340
to be uploaded to the Container
Registry because, of course,

00:15:39.340 --> 00:15:41.700
Docker has the property that
each layer has a checksum,

00:15:41.700 --> 00:15:44.020
and if the checksums match, it
doesn't need to do any work.

00:15:44.020 --> 00:15:46.478
So the incrementality benefits
can go all the way from your

00:15:46.478 --> 00:15:50.270
build through to
your deployment.

00:15:50.270 --> 00:15:52.590
And so I have a quick
video showing that.

00:15:59.090 --> 00:16:01.760
So this is our
example application.

00:16:01.760 --> 00:16:03.920
And you see there's
some text there.

00:16:03.920 --> 00:16:05.850
The live edit and deploy.

00:16:05.850 --> 00:16:08.030
And so we're going to make
a change to that text.

00:16:10.023 --> 00:16:11.690
I should point out
while this is running

00:16:11.690 --> 00:16:13.730
that all the demos
you've seen today

00:16:13.730 --> 00:16:15.000
were actually Angular itself.

00:16:15.000 --> 00:16:16.715
And so those 174 tests
Danna was running

00:16:16.715 --> 00:16:17.840
was the Angular repository.

00:16:17.840 --> 00:16:20.007
That was a demo of our
actual day-to-day development

00:16:20.007 --> 00:16:22.350
experience.

00:16:22.350 --> 00:16:24.530
So now we're going to
run deploy dot replace.

00:16:24.530 --> 00:16:27.080
So the deploy target
is a Kubernetes target.

00:16:27.080 --> 00:16:28.940
This is going to go
to Kubernetes Engine,

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:31.070
and the replace is just a verb
saying what we want to do.

00:16:31.070 --> 00:16:32.528
If we were making
a new application

00:16:32.528 --> 00:16:33.830
we would say create.

00:16:33.830 --> 00:16:37.160
And so Bazel, as you
can see, 13,000 actions

00:16:37.160 --> 00:16:38.090
didn't need to be run.

00:16:38.090 --> 00:16:40.388
So we got a lot of
cache benefits now.

00:16:40.388 --> 00:16:41.930
And then the remaining
part, the work

00:16:41.930 --> 00:16:42.830
that's still happening
here, is we're

00:16:42.830 --> 00:16:44.243
bundling up the application.

00:16:44.243 --> 00:16:46.910
We're running some slow steps to
do the production optimizations

00:16:46.910 --> 00:16:49.910
for this application, and
it is a medium scale app.

00:16:49.910 --> 00:16:52.130
So that takes some time.

00:16:52.130 --> 00:16:54.020
And then once the
app is fully built,

00:16:54.020 --> 00:16:57.890
Bazel knows how to construct
the Docker image for it.

00:16:57.890 --> 00:17:00.440
And so now we see this
image layer is running.

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:03.623
It's running too quickly for
me to talk over, which is good.

00:17:03.623 --> 00:17:05.540
And then the gzip happened,
and so now there's

00:17:05.540 --> 00:17:06.740
a kubectl command running.

00:17:06.740 --> 00:17:09.694
This is the command line utility
that deploys to Kubernetes.

00:17:09.694 --> 00:17:11.569
So again, I did all of
this with one command,

00:17:11.569 --> 00:17:14.700
and I didn't need to say what
needed to be built first.

00:17:14.700 --> 00:17:16.310
I just said, just
like redeploy me.

00:17:16.310 --> 00:17:18.270
I don't know what changed.

00:17:18.270 --> 00:17:20.270
So now we're going to
wait for Kubernetes Engine

00:17:20.270 --> 00:17:22.187
to spin up some new
workers with the new code.

00:17:22.187 --> 00:17:24.079
There's a load
balancer in front,

00:17:24.079 --> 00:17:26.572
and so now that LiveEdit
shows up in the application.

00:17:30.440 --> 00:17:31.603
So that's all we have.

00:17:31.603 --> 00:17:33.770
We're happy to answer some
questions after the talk.

00:17:33.770 --> 00:17:35.430
We'll be over here in this area.

00:17:35.430 --> 00:17:37.220
Thank you very much.

00:17:37.220 --> 00:17:37.820
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:37.820 --> 00:17:42.670
[MUSIC PLAYING]

